MMALDecode - H.264 decoded by the VideoCore, from RISC OS
=========================================================

Hardware H.264 decoding for Reel on the Raspberry Pi: the firmware's
video decoder, driven through RISC OS's VCHIQ module. 0.12 decoded
whole clips correctly (1080p with gpu_mem=128). 0.13 and 0.14 try what
a player needs on top of that (0.14 fixes 0.13's reading of FFmpeg's
times, which failed every MP4 check though the decoder was right):

  - MP4 input: each picture's data goes to the decoder on its own, with
    its time (pts), as FFmpeg and Reel will send it;
  - display order: clips with B frames are decoded in one order and shown
    in another; each picture must come back with its own time, in the
    order it's shown;
  - flush and seek: stopping part way through and going on from a
    keyframe, as a seek in a player does;
  - timing without the checks: how fast pictures come back, and how
    long copying each one out of the decoder's memory takes.

The clips (in clips; each as mp4 and as raw H.264, stream):
  small  640x360, 60 pictures, H.264 Main with B frames, a keyframe
         every 20
  odd    426x240, 30 pictures, H.264 Baseline (a width that isn't a
         multiple of 32, to check the cropping)
  hd     1920x1080, 150 pictures, H.264 High, level 4.1, B frames

Each picture is checked against a checksum FFmpeg made of the same
picture with its own decoder. The VideoCore's pictures can differ from
FFmpeg's by 1 in a few values (rounding), so each is also compared by
block averages: "within rounding" is fine, anything more is a fault.

Running it (please send back every Result file):
  Test       The small clip as raw H.264 (as 0.12 sent it), then all
             three clips from their MP4s, checked by time. Writes
             Result. If 1080p stalls, set gpu_mem=128 in CONFIG/TXT
             (see below).
  Seek       The small clip; after 10 pictures it flushes the decoder
             and carries on from the keyframe at picture 40. Writes
             every message to ResultS.
  SeekD      As Seek, but it switches the decoder's ports off and on
             again instead of flushing. Writes ResultSD. Run it even if
             Seek works: they're two ways Reel could seek.
  Speed      The three MP4s without checks: pictures a second, and the
             copying time, two ways (word by word, and in blocks).
             Writes ResultT.
  Verbose    The small MP4 with every message (ResultV, and the first
             pictures in Dump), if Test doesn't get pictures back.
  VerboseHD  The same for the hd clip (ResultHD and DumpHD).

  Each report starts by saying how much memory the VideoCore has
  (gpu_mem in CONFIG/TXT). 1080p needs gpu_mem=128 or more: at 64 the
  decoder stalls without a word.

The decoded pictures come back to RISC OS by the VCHIQ module's bulk
transfer, and a nine-instruction callback in the RMA counts them in; it's
removed at the end. If the machine locks up (it shouldn't), switch it off
and on again.

If a run stops part way (the report says "no reply"), the VideoCore's
decoder can stay stuck until the machine is restarted: restart before
running anything else.

Needs: RISC OS 5 on a Raspberry Pi, with the VCHIQ module (in the ROM).
Part of riscos-reelhwaccel; GPL version 2 or later. The clips are made
from FFmpeg's testsrc2 pattern.
